> I am writing up a methods appendix for a report. The first part of this section discusses risk modeling:
> ```
> We estimate labour productivity impacts using two complementary sources: a meta-analysis of experimental exposure–response functions from \citetalias{gosling2018peseta}, and econometric estimates from \citetalias{schleypen2019}. Both are translated into temperature-dependent damage functions for the UK and combined via the same meta-analytic procedure applied in other sectors.
> 
> \citetalias{gosling2018peseta} summarise five underlying productivity models as exposure–response functions (ERFs) that relate work capacity to wet-bulb temperature, based on experimental and physiological evidence for heat stress. These ERFs are applied to outdoor labour and heat-exposed sectors to obtain UK-wide losses in effective labour productivity under alternative climate scenarios. We fit a reduced-form UK-level damage function that maps global mean temperature (and time, conditional on SSP1-2.6 and SSP3-7.0) to percentage losses in output attributable to reduced labour productivity. Following our standard downscaling procedure, we project this function to ADM3 level and rescale regional results, weighted by population, to ensure they aggregate back to the UK-wide values.
> 
> \citetalias{schleypen2019} take an econometric approach, estimating how sectoral value added from labour responds to temperature, using historical variation rather than experimental data. We extract the implied UK temperature–output relationship, convert it to percentage changes in sectoral value added per degree of warming, and aggregate these to a national measure of labour-related output losses. This yields a second reduced-form damage function, again expressed as a mapping from global mean temperature to annual UK losses (in monetary terms and as a share of GDP).
> 
> The two resulting damage functions—one rooted in experimental/physiological constraints, the other in observed economic responses—are then combined using a simple Bayesian meta-analysis. Each model is treated as a noisy observation of an underlying, unknown labour-productivity damage function. The combined function provides central estimates and uncertainty ranges for UK labour productivity losses as a percentage of GDP. The general meta-analytic framework and its implementation across sectors are described in \citet{rising2026comprehensive} and are not repeated here.
