> We represent labour adaptation as a set of interventions that reduce heat-related productivity losses at the UK level and allow comparison of their marginal effectiveness per pound spent.
> 
> We first construct an indicative portfolio of workplace adaptation options: mechanical cooling, solar blinds, increased mechanical ventilation, and behavioural changes (e.g., work–rest cycles and flexible working). For each option, we specify (i) a baseline monetised annual loss from heat-related productivity impacts in the absence of adaptation, and (ii) the annual losses averted under full deployment, expressed in monetary terms. The ratio of averted to baseline losses defines the maximum fraction of national labour-productivity damages that can be eliminated by that option.
> 
> Capital and operating costs for the building-related adaptations are expressed on a floor-area basis. Capital costs per unit area are annualised over a ten-year repayment period, and operating costs per unit area reflect estimated electricity consumption for cooling and ventilation combined with UK non-domestic electricity prices (CIBSE cooling benchmarks and UK energy price statistics; new reference). To obtain national totals, we compile local-authority floorspace estimates, combining observed commercial floorspace data where available with a regression-based prediction elsewhere. Specifically, we estimate UK non-domestic floorspace for each county $i$ as a function of population, and use this relationship to impute floorspace in counties without direct observations. Aggregating over all counties yields total non-domestic floorspace, which we multiply by annualised per–square metre costs and, where appropriate, by assumed penetration rates to obtain an effective annual UK cost for each adaptation option.
> 
> For each adaptation measure $k$, we then calculate a national “cost-effectiveness” measure: the percentage reduction in UK labour-productivity losses per year, divided by the annual UK expenditure on that measure. This yields a marginal effect expressed as
> \[
> \frac{\mathrm{d} L}{\mathrm{d} C_k} \approx \frac{\Delta L_k}{C_k},
> \]
> where $L$ is the share of GDP lost to heat-related labour productivity impacts and $C_k$ is annual UK spending on measure $k$. These point estimates for building adaptations enter the marginal adaptation curve as horizontal segments at the corresponding spending levels.
> 
> Heat-health alert systems are treated as a continuous, scalable adaptation. We parameterise the annual cost of operating a national alert system by extrapolating published Scottish cost estimates to the UK population and interpolating between future years using a log-linear model of total costs over time (national meteorological service cost-effectiveness study; new reference). For a given year $t$ and annual spending level $C$, we assume that the share of the population effectively covered by alerts follows an increasing but saturating relationship,
> \[
> \pi_t(C) = 1 - \exp(-\alpha_t C),
> \]
> with $\alpha_t$ calibrated such that spending equal to an estimated “full system” cost in year $t$ achieves $95\%$ effective coverage.
> 
> The effectiveness of heat alerts in reducing labour losses is quantified from an empirical study of UK workers’ responses to heat warnings \citep{robinsonYYYY} (Robinson worker productivity study; new reference). We interpret the reported coefficients on heatwave occurrence and warning exposure as describing proportional changes in working hours and effort. For a given heat anomaly and alert coverage $\pi_t(C)$, we simulate $m$ realisations of hours and effort responses, deriving a distribution of residual productivity losses relative to a no-alert baseline. The resulting expected fraction of remaining loss, $f_t(C)$, defines the risk reduction attributable to alerts as $1 - f_t(C)$.
> 
> To characterise the marginal impact of an additional pound spent on the alert system at spending level $C$, we compute a finite-difference approximation,
> \[
> \frac{\mathrm{d} L_t}{\mathrm{d} C}\bigg\rvert_C \approx -\frac{f_t(C+\delta) - f_t(C)}{\delta} \times 100,
> \]
> for a small increment $\delta$, scaled to give the percentage-point reduction in national labour-productivity losses per additional pound spent. Repeating this procedure across a range of annual spending levels and for illustrative heat anomalies (e.g., $1$, $2$, and $3\,^\circ$C) and future years $t$ yields a set of smooth marginal-effect curves for heat alerts.
> 
> Finally, we combine the discrete building-adaptation points with the continuous alert-system curves to produce an overall marginal adaptation cost curve for labour productivity. This curve reports, on log–log axes, the percentage reduction in UK labour-productivity losses per million pounds of annual spending against the scale of that spending, allowing comparison across adaptation solutions and across different levels of climate stress. The resulting figure is shown as Figure~\ref{fig:labour-mac-labour}.
> 
> \begin{figure}[t]
>   \centering
>   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figures/adapt/labour/mac-labour.pdf}
>   \caption{Marginal effectiveness of labour-related adaptation measures in reducing UK heat-related productivity losses. Curves show heat alert systems under alternative temperature anomalies and future years; points show building-level adaptations, with the legend indicating the “Adaptation Solution”.}
>   \label{fig:labour-mac-labour}
> \end{figure}
