> Given a specified national adaptation budget, we allocate spending dynamically across years and interventions and apply the resulting adaptations to the unadapted risk projections. For each year $t$ and climate scenario, we first determine the spending on heat alerts required to achieve a fixed effective coverage of $95\%$ of the UK population. If the available annual budget (expressed in million pounds per year and converted to pounds) exceeds this amount, the remaining funds are allocated to the building-level adaptations in proportion to their national marginal cost-effectiveness values. This allocation rule implies that the share of residual funds directed to each building option $k$ is proportional to its estimated reduction in percentage losses per pound, so that the combined package approximates the upper envelope of the marginal adaptation cost curve.
> 
> The impact of the resulting adaptation mix on productivity is evaluated using projected UK temperature trajectories. For each scenario–year combination, we compute a national heat anomaly from UK-average temperature and, for each of several temperature quantiles (central estimate and bounds), simulate $m$ Monte Carlo draws of work hours and effort with and without alerts in place. The alert system is assumed to apply uniformly across the UK with $95\%$ effective coverage. We then combine these simulated proportional changes in hours and effort with population-weighting across England and the rest of the UK to obtain national-scale measures of remaining effective labour supply under the alert system alone. Building-level adaptations are represented as an additional proportional reduction in these residual losses: the share of loss removed by the building package is taken to be equal to the aggregate effectiveness implied by the allocated spending, obtained by summing over interventions the product of allocated spending and their marginal cost-effectiveness. This aggregate “cooling” effect is subtracted from the alert-adjusted loss, yielding, for each year $t$ and scenario, adapted projections of total hours and effort as fractions of the no-heat-loss baseline. These adapted productivity paths are then mapped back into monetary terms using the reduced-form damage functions described above.
