> We incorporate interannual variability and low-frequency extremes into the labour damages by combining climate-model ensemble spread, econometric residual uncertainty, and a simple stochastic representation of “bad years” at both subnational and national scales.
> 
> For variability in lost labour hours, we start from daily temperature projections for each first-level administrative unit and climate-model run. For each county $i$, model realisation, and year $t$ between 2025 and 2075, we compute a corresponding heat anomaly from annual mean temperature and translate this into projected hours of lost labour per worker using the experimental heat–productivity response with and without partial alert coverage (assumed to reach $50\%$ of the population). This yields annual series of unadapted and partially adapted hours lost for each model run. We then add an independent stochastic component to represent unexplained interannual variation in hours lost, calibrated so that the additional noise contributes a plausible share of overall variance (following the Heckman-corrected specification in \citealp{robinsonYYYY}; Robinson worker productivity study, new reference). Expected annual damages (EAD) in hours per worker are obtained, for each year and climate scenario, by aggregating across model runs to form ensemble means and empirical $5$th–$95$th percentile ranges at the county level.
> 
> To represent rare extremes in hours lost, we focus on a high-emissions climate pathway and, for selected future years, construct multi-year windows of annual outcomes. We add the same stochastic residual to each year’s lost hours, and identify, for each ensemble member, the single “worst” year within each window as the one with the greatest loss of labour time. These worst-year outcomes are then summarised by county as means and percentile bands, and interpreted as 1-in-200 labour-loss events at that horizon. We compute both county-level results and population-weighted UK averages, using subnational population data to aggregate hours per worker.
> 
> For monetary damages, we proceed analogously using the reduced-form damage functions described above. For each county $i$, climate-model run, and target period, we generate distributions of county-level labour damages in million pounds based on the structural uncertainty of the temperature–productivity regression. We then interpolate in time to obtain annual damage series from 2025 to 2075 for each run, climate scenario, and county. Ensemble EAD paths are constructed by taking, for each year and scenario, the cross-run mean and percentile range of county-level damages. To superimpose additional year-to-year economic variability consistent with the underlying econometric fit, we calibrate a multiplicative normal disturbance so that the ratio of explained to total variance in damages matches the reported coefficient of determination from \citet{rode2022} (Rode labour-temperature econometric study; new reference). Specifically, we estimate a simple predictive model for expected county damages and then determine the standard deviation of the residual term such that the resulting total variance reproduces the target $R^2$. This yields a stochastic residual process that can be added to the expected damages for each county and year.
> 
> We use this residual process to construct extreme monetary-loss years under the high-emissions scenario. For each ensemble member and 10-year window starting at a given year, we add the calibrated residual to the expected damages for each county and year, aggregate across counties to obtain a national total in each year, and then select the year of maximum national damage as the extreme outcome for that ensemble member. The distribution of these worst-year totals across ensemble members defines the mean and percentile bounds of 1-in-200 national losses for that starting year. Annual EAD and extreme-year outcomes are reported both at the county scale and aggregated to the whole UK.
> 
> The resulting variability and extreme-event patterns are illustrated in four figures. Figure~\ref{fig:labour-dataset-adm1-ukwide} compares UK-average EAD in lost hours per worker to extreme-hour events over time, under both a most-likely and a high-risk climate pathway. Figure~\ref{fig:labour-dataset-adm1} shows the corresponding distributions at the county scale in hours per worker, while Figures~\ref{fig:labour-dataset-adm3} and \ref{fig:labour-dataset-uk} present analogous results in monetary units at the county and UK scales, respectively.
> 
