> We superimpose stochastic annual variability and rare extremes on the smooth, scenario-mean labour damage paths described above, using both climate-model spread and econometric residual uncertainty.
> 
> For lost labour hours, we use daily-temperature projections from a multi-model ensemble at first-level administrative units. For each county $i$, ensemble member, and year $t$ (2025–2075), we convert annual mean temperature to a heat anomaly and apply the experimental heat–productivity response, with and without partial alert coverage (assumed at $50\%$), to obtain unadapted and partially adapted hours lost per worker. We add an independent normal residual to these hours to represent unexplained year-to-year variability, scaled so that the stochastic term contributes a realistic fraction of total variance (calibrated to the empirical study of worker responses to heat alerts; \citealp{robinsonYYYY}, Robinson worker productivity study, new reference). For each year, climate pathway, and county, we then compute expected annual damages (EAD) and $5$th–$95$th percentile intervals from the ensemble; UK-wide values are obtained using population-weighted averages across counties.
> 
> To represent rare extremes, we focus on a high-risk climate pathway. For selected start years, we take 10-year windows of annual hours lost (including the stochastic residual) and, for each ensemble member, identify the single worst year (maximum loss) in the window. These “worst-year” values define, for each county and nationally, the mean and percentile range of 1-in-200 outcomes, reported alongside the EAD paths.
> 
> For monetary damages, we follow the same structure but use the reduced-form damage projections. For each county, scenario, and ensemble member, we time-interpolate damages to annual values. We then estimate a simple predictive model of expected damages and calibrate a multiplicative normal residual so that the resulting total variance matches the reported $R^2$ of the underlying econometric fit \citep{rode2022} (Rode labour–temperature econometric study, new reference). This residual is added to expected damages to obtain annual stochastic realisations, from which we construct EAD and 1-in-200 outcomes at county and UK scales using the same decadal “worst-year” procedure as for hours.
